    },
    {
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "slide": 1,
      "expression": "smug",
      "text": "Subtitles translated 우리는 깐부잖아 as just friends. That completely erases the drama.",
      "delivery": "scoffing"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "slide": 2,
      "expression": "explaining",
      "text": "First, 우리는 means as for us, setting you both up as one united side.",
      "delivery": "instructive"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "learner",
      "slide": 3,
      "expression": "confused",
      "text": "Okay, but what actually is a 깐부 anyway?",
      "delivery": "curious"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "slide": 3,
      "expression": "explaining",
      "text": "깐부 is neighborhood marble slang. It means partners who share all their marbles.",
      "delivery": "warm"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "slide": 4,
      "expression": "explaining",
      "text": "And 잖아 means you know. It reminds someone of a shared fact.",
      "delivery": "pointed"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "learner",
      "slide": 5,
      "expression": "surprised",
      "text": "So it is not a casual reminder, it is a guilt trip!",
      "delivery": "gasping"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "slide": 5,
      "expression": "smug",
      "text": "Exactly. It really means: we are ride-or-die, you cannot turn on me!",
      "delivery": "intense"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "slide": 6,
      "expression": "happy",
      "text": "Use that ending when nagging, like 내일 시험이잖아!",
